## Pipeline Monitoring Checks

The monitoring logic checks pipeline health across several areas.

### Pipeline Run Status

Checks whether pipeline runs completed successfully.

Example statuses:

```text
Succeeded
Failed
Cancelled
Skipped
No Data
```

Business value:

```text
Helps identify whether reporting datasets are safe to refresh or need review.
```

---

### SLA Breach Detection

Checks whether a pipeline started late or ran longer than expected.

Example:

```text
Expected start time: 5:00 AM
Actual start time: 6:20 AM
SLA threshold: 60 minutes
Status: Late Start
```

Business value:

```text
Helps detect delayed dashboard refresh risk before users consume reports.
```

---

### Zero-Record Load Detection

Checks whether a pipeline loaded zero records when records were expected.

Example:

```text
Pipeline: inventory_current_load
Records loaded: 0
Status: Review Required
```

Business value:

```text
Helps prevent dashboards from refreshing with missing or incomplete data.
```

---

## Data Quality Checks

### Source-to-Target Row Count Validation

Compares the number of records from the source system against the number of records loaded into the target layer.

Example:

```text
Source rows: 1,250,000
Target rows: 1,249,980
Difference: 20 records
Status: Review
```

Business value:

```text
Helps confirm whether the pipeline loaded complete data.
```

---

### Duplicate Check

Identifies duplicate records that could inflate reporting metrics.

Example:

```text
Duplicate key:
product_id + store_id + sales_date
```

Business value:

```text
Prevents sales, inventory, or PO metrics from being overstated.
```

---

### Null / Mandatory Field Check

Validates that critical fields are populated.

Example mandatory fields:

```text
product_id
store_id
sales_date
po_number
inventory_load_date
```

Business value:

```text
Ensures reporting datasets contain the required fields for dashboard logic.
```

---

### Schema Drift Detection

Identifies when expected columns are missing, renamed, or changed in data type.

Example:

```text
Expected column: sales_amount
Expected data type: numeric
Actual data type: string
Status: Data Type Mismatch
```

Business value:

```text
Helps prevent downstream model failures when source structures change.
```

---

## Issue Severity Logic

Not every issue has the same business impact. The framework classifies issues based on severity.

Example severity levels:

```text
Critical
High
Medium
Low
```

Example classification:

```text
Critical:
A business-critical pipeline failed or a required source column is missing.

High:
A row count mismatch is above tolerance or a schema data type changed.

Medium:
A zero-record load occurred or a nullable field changed.

Low:
A minor delay or non-critical warning occurred.
```

This helps teams prioritize which issues need immediate review.

